title: Linux host-scoped IP defaults break multi-rail RDMA on Kubernetes
source: hn
url: https://oguzpastirmaci.com/posts/arp-rp-filter-and-accept-ra-for-rdma-pods-on-kubernetes/
date: '2026-11-03'
authors:
- asaiacai
why_read: Understand how default Linux networking behaviors disrupt multi-rail RDMA workloads in GPU Kubernetes clusters. You will learn the specific sysctl configurations and namespace placements needed to resolve silent networking failures.
hn_id: '50357029'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50357029
image: /infographics/01-hn-50357029.jpg
---

Running distributed training across sixteen-rail RoCE GPU nodes exposes a nasty quirk in the default Linux network stack. Linux implements a weak host model where IP addresses belong to the entire host rather than specific network interfaces.

When sixteen RoCE interfaces share a subnet, ARP requests arriving on one rail will gladly trigger responses from any other rail. The result is total chaos for collective communications: NCCL aborts with missing IPv6 addresses, peer addresses resolve to wrong physical MACs, and bandwidth benchmarks fail intermittently across arbitrary rails.

Fixing this requires setting arp_ignore to one and arp_announce to two, ensuring each interface answers only for its own IP and announces the matching source. You must also disable rp_filter to prevent reverse path drops across asymmetric paths, and configure accept_ra properly for IPv6 routing advertisements.

The real engineering headache on Kubernetes is lifecycle ownership. Depending on whether you allocate interfaces via SR-IOV device plugins, Multus, or host device injection, these sysctls must be tuned inside the pod network namespace before the training workload starts.

High-speed networking failures in machine learning infrastructure are almost never hardware bugs; they are standard operating system defaults operating at unprecedented scale.

