ByteDance redesigned its storage architecture around a severe data asymmetry: millions of continuous streaming writes colliding with massive, repetitive batch reads for machine learning inference. Standard LSM key-value stores like RocksDB buckle when serving long user action sequences because repeated compaction cycles waste input-output bandwidth on data that training models scan repeatedly.

The proposed system, USDB, solves this by introducing CascadeLSM, which aligns compaction stages directly with temporal user activity windows. Rather than duplicating work across streaming ingestion, live inference, and offline training pipelines, differentiated replicas share underlying persistent files. Storage nodes use a hybrid columnar layout that enables zero-copy projection reads, while partial dumps reconstruct recent feature states on demand.

Production deployment metrics reveal an 85.8 percent reduction in mean write latency alongside a 42.1 percent drop in infrastructure expenditures. Furthermore, pushing aggregation operators directly into the storage layer delivered an 8.5-fold throughput improvement over standard scan routines under constrained bandwidth.

Specializing storage layouts for model consumption patterns delivers efficiency gains that generic key-value engines cannot match.

