title: Physical address homing determines core to core latency variance
source: hn
url: https://jacen.li/writings/core-to-core-is-not-core-to-core/
date: '2026-11-03'
authors:
- Jacen Li
why_read: Read this to understand how physical address hashing across distributed cache slices impacts core-to-core latency. You will learn why inter-core communication latency varies significantly and how coherency homing affects performance.
hn_id: '50357294'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50357294
---

Core-to-core latency benchmarks often report a single static number between two processor cores, but that average hides a critical hardware reality. In modern mesh and ringbus architectures, latency between the exact same pair of cores can swing between 40 nanoseconds and 60 nanoseconds depending entirely on the physical memory address being accessed.

This behavior occurs because cache coherency is resolved at designated home slices distributed across the interconnect mesh. L3 cache is divided into per-tile slices, and every physical address hashes to exactly one specific coherence manager tile. If core A and core B sit on one corner of the mesh, but the line hashes to a home agent located on the far opposite corner, every coherency handoff must traverse the entire chip topology.

Engineers optimizing lock-free queues, ring buffers, or high-throughput thread handoffs can exploit this mechanism directly. By steering allocation addresses toward localized homes, you eliminate unnecessary hops across the on-die fabric.

Microbenchmarks that measure core-to-core latency with a single fixed pointer miss this variance entirely.

