title: ZFS provides write ordering without expensive fsync disk flushes
source: hn
url: https://blog.dfb.sh/blog/zfs-fsync/
date: '2026-11-03'
authors:
- deafbeef
why_read: Read this to understand how disabling sync in ZFS provides write ordering without expensive flushes, dramatically reducing disk write latency.
hn_id: '50352786'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50352786
---

Every standard fsync call in ext4 forces two distinct guarantees together: ordering and durability. When SQLite or any embedded store issues an fsync, ext4 asks jbd2 to commit the active running transaction and immediately flushes the hardware disk cache. On a busy spinning drive or mirrored array, a one-byte write can stall behind gigabytes of unrelated I/O, spiking write latencies past 16 milliseconds.

Many backend workloads do not actually need immediate physical durability on every single write. They only require strict ordering across crashes. If a server loses power, rolling back thirty seconds of mirrored cache state is often acceptable, but corrupting structural metadata is catastrophic.

This is where ZFS offers an elegant operational compromise. Setting sync to disabled in ZFS preserves transaction group ordering without blocking application threads on disk cache flushes. Write operations drop from 16 milliseconds to 2 milliseconds, while disk utilization falls from 13 percent to under 3 percent.

Decoupling ordering from physical persistence turns storage bottlenecks into predictable latency curves.

