Reasoning models frequently get caught in degenerative thought loops, repeatedly generating hesitation tokens such as "Wait" until they exhaust their context windows. In investigating Qwen3.5-0.8B, researchers used a Jacobian lens to inspect the residual stream across all 24 layers at the exact inflection token before a loop triggers.

The findings show that standard abliteration - simply projecting out the looping direction once - only reduces loop frequencies by roughly one-third. In contrast, an aggressive weight update that subtracted that direction six times over across 64 neurons in two specific layers reduced reasoning loops by 86 percent and lifted MATH-500 accuracy from 7.9% to 48.1%.

Looking into the internal representations reveals the mechanism: removing the looping direction allows downstream attention heads to surface tokens like "Verification" and "Completion" instead of lingering in doubt.

Targeted linear weight interventions provide a viable alternative to compute-heavy reinforcement learning when resolving repetitive failure modes in small reasoning models.

