why_read: Learn how Grouped Value Attention reconstructs content keys on demand to reduce KV cache memory by nearly half with minimal accuracy loss. It provides a practical architectural technique to overcome memory-bandwidth bottlenecks in transformer inference.
hn_id: '50350790'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50350790
---

Serving long-context language models in production is primarily a memory bandwidth and KV cache capacity problem. While Grouped-Query Attention (GQA) dramatically cuts memory bandwidth by sharing key-value heads across multiple query heads, it still mandates storing explicit key and value tensors for every token in the sequence.

Grouped Value Attention (GVA) challenges this baseline by caching only grouped values and dynamically reconstructing content keys via a learned linear map. During decoding, this reconstruction map is absorbed directly into the query projection, entirely removing the need to materialize or store content keys in the decode path. Positional relationships are retained using a compact, separately cached decoupled RoPE channel.

In empirical benchmarks at the 350M parameter scale trained on 30B tokens, this architecture slashes persistent KV cache scalar footprints by 45 to 47 percent relative to matched GQA. Crucially, downstream accuracy holds steady at 44.18 versus 44.36 for GQA, outpacing MLA baselines.

Shrinking the cache footprint without sacrificing model quality changes the economics of long-context inference clusters.

