Parsing high-throughput network streams often hits a wall not in memory bandwidth, but in register allocation overhead and function call dispatch. In high-performance systems like Protobuf parsers, dispatching tokens via guaranteed tail calls consistently beats traditional switch loops by allowing the compiler to optimize register sequences across hops.

However, existing LLVM calling conventions like tailcc introduce ABI frictions and poor register preservation patterns. The proposed tail_chaincc convention combines callee-cleanup stack management with the register optimization mechanics of preserve_none. This change eliminates redundant register spills and restores at tail dispatch points.

For systems engineers writing high-speed network protocols, serialization libraries, or bytecode interpreters, this RFC represents a serious performance leap. It turns guaranteed tail recursion into a zero-cost abstraction for state machine dispatch.

Treating control flow as tail-call chains rather than giant switch statements may finally deliver the raw machine efficiency it always promised.

