---

When a MongoDB replica set grows beyond the WiredTiger cache, latency stops reflecting query complexity and begins tracking disk seek times. The standard industry advice is to shard the collection, but sharding introduces massive operational complexity: immutable shard keys, config servers, mongos routers, and cross-shard balancing overhead.

In many production systems, the vast majority of read traffic touches only recently created documents, while historical data clogs the cache. Percona explored an architectural middle ground called Thermocline, a transparent storage-tiering proxy designed to sit between the application and MongoDB.

Instead of full sharding, a compact, memory-fit replica set handles recent writes and hot reads, while the legacy cluster acts as an archival tier. The proxy routes queries transparently based on document age without requiring any application-level changes or costly schema redesigns.

Before taking on the irreversible operational baggage of a distributed shard cluster, look closely at your data access temperature.

