hn_id: '50354432'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50354432
---

Standard tool calling in AI agents is hitting an architectural wall caused by turn-by-turn round-tripping. Forcing an LLM to emit JSON for one tool, wait for execution, re-inject the payload into the context window, and re-sample creates massive latency and token bloat.

The industry is rapidly shifting toward code mode, replacing dozens of granular tool schemas with a single sandboxed execution interface. Instead of issuing three round trips to search, fetch, and parse data, the model writes a short Python or TypeScript script that chains those dependent actions in one pass.

This collapses intermediate network calls and prevents verbose intermediate outputs from polluting prompt history. Sandboxed script execution lets the runtime handle error recovery, filtering, and concurrency directly within the worker environment.

The future of agent tooling is not designing more JSON schemas, but exposing clean APIs to an isolated code interpreter.
